  "title": "Nitration of benzene",
  "caption": "π electrons from the delocalised ring attack the nitronium ion, NO₂⁺, giving the arenium (Wheland) intermediate: the horseshoe shows the delocalisation broken at the sp³ carbon, with the positive charge spread over the other five, which is why this step is slow. HSO₄⁻ then removes H⁺ from the sp³ carbon, the C–H bonding pair restores the aromatic ring, and the catalyst H₂SO₄ is regenerated. The arrow from an N=O bond onto oxygen shows where nitrogen's extra bond comes from; A-level answers usually show only the ring-to-N arrow and write the product –NO₂.",
  "alt": "Electrophilic aromatic substitution: benzene attacks the nitronium ion to form an arenium ion, then hydrogensulfate removes a proton to give nitrobenzene and sulfuric acid.",
  "description": [
    "Stage 1: benzene drawn as a hexagon with a circle inside (delocalised), the nitronium ion O=N⁺=O above and to the right, and a hydrogensulfate ion to the left. A curly arrow goes from the circle to the nitrogen, and another from one N=O bond onto that oxygen.",
    "Stage 2: the arenium ion. The top carbon is sp³, bonded to a hydrogen and the nitro group; a horseshoe covers the other five carbons with a + inside. A curly arrow goes from a lone pair on hydrogensulfate's O⁻ to that hydrogen, and another from the C–H bond back into the ring.",
    "Stage 3: nitrobenzene, with the delocalised ring (circle) restored, and sulfuric acid, H₂SO₄."
  ],
